                    briefing_text_r1:       `The WOMS pilot leaked. Staff now know the system scores them in real time and that managers discussed using the analytics for performance and “right-sizing.” You hold the capability spec, the adoption survey (only <strong>38% willing</strong>), the union's letter and a POPIA summary. Resist the temptation to treat WOMS as a neutral efficiency tool: it reshapes <strong>power, visibility and trust</strong> between managers and workers. Map the social consequences and name the single biggest risk — the <em>social subsystem rejecting the technical one</em>: gaming, work-to-rule and a union dispute that turns the ZAR 95M gain into a loss.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Set the data-use rules`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Decide what the system may and may not do. The hinge is <strong>purpose limitation</strong>: coordination & safety, individual performance management, or headcount decisions — they are not the same choice. Set who sees individual-level data, whether scores drive automated decisions, retention, and the <strong>POPIA lawful basis and DPIA</strong>. Allow the analytics to drive performance and “right-sizing” and you process employees' data for a purpose they were never told about — a POPIA breach — and <em>every worker treats the system as a threat and feeds it the minimum</em>. Switch the module off entirely and you forfeit the fatigue-and-safety detection too. Govern the dual-use system; don't just avoid it.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Choose the change & adoption approach`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Design the rollout against the board's 6-week deadline. Sequence the 9 DCs (phased vs. big-bang), choose your consultation model, and fund training and transparency. Adoption is <strong>co-produced</strong>: a system done <em>to</em> people, not <em>with</em> them, is resisted however well it works. Skip the union and you hand them a legitimate grievance for free — what would two weeks of genuine consultation have cost versus a national dispute? Then commit a target adoption rate to the board that your levers can actually support — trust, once lost, rebuilds slowly.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Install an ethics safeguard & defend the rollout`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Choose <strong>one binding</strong> ethics safeguard and present the integrated plan to a board-and-union forum. A charter on the intranet with no human-in-the-loop, no appeal and no independent review is <em>symbolic, not binding</em> — the first time a worker is disciplined by a score they can't see or contest, it's exposed as PR. Make the safeguard real: an enforceable appeal, human review of individual decisions, independent oversight, worker representation. Then defend the plan — reconcile the ZAR 95M efficiency target, POPIA compliance, union relations and trust into one honest case.`,

