                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_socio:         `<strong>That's the right lens.</strong> WOMS is a socio-technical system — it shifts power, visibility and trust, and your real decisions are social and ethical, not just technical. Now design the social subsystem alongside the technical one, or the 38% won't move.`,
                    coach_r1_neutral:       `<strong>“Just an efficiency tool” is Common Error number one.</strong> The pilot already leaked that it scores people in real time — workers don't experience it as neutral, and treating adoption as a training afterthought is how the social subsystem rejects the technical one.`,
                    coach_r1_technical:     `<strong>A stable integration that nobody uses as intended is still a failure.</strong> The hard part here isn't the technology — it's the trust the technology just put at risk. The people side doesn't follow the system; it decides whether the system works.`,
                    coach_r1_cost:          `<strong>If the ZAR 95M is the only thing you can see, you'll lose it.</strong> The efficiency case depends entirely on adoption, and adoption depends on trust. Framing surveillance worries as comms noise is exactly how you hand the journalist the story.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You read it as a socio-technical system AND named the real risk — the workforce rejecting the tool through gaming, work-to-rule and a dispute. That is the lens that lets the next three decisions earn adoption instead of imposing it.`,
                    coach_r1_contained:     `<strong>“Each issue is survivable alone” is what the journalist hopes you believe.</strong> Adoption, the union and POPIA compound: surveillance perception kills adoption, which feeds the dispute, which becomes the headline. They are one social risk, not three.`,
                    coach_r1_notlistening:  `<strong>You can't read a social system you won't listen to.</strong> Without genuinely hearing frontline staff and supervisors now, you'll design rules and a rollout for a workforce you've only imagined — and the 38% will tell you so at go-live.`,
                    coach_r2_coord:         `<strong>Purpose limitation done right.</strong> Coordination and safety only — never individual performance or headcount — is the line that lets workers trust the system instead of gaming it. That single rule is worth more adoption than any comms campaign.`,
                    coach_r2_perf:          `<strong>A middle line, but watch it.</strong> Using individual scores for performance management — even without headcount — still tells workers the system measures them to judge them. You've kept some surveillance perception; the trust cost is real even if smaller.`,
                    coach_r2_hr:            `<strong>You permitted the data for performance and “right-sizing.”</strong> Legally, that's processing employees' personal data for a purpose they were never told about — a POPIA breach. Practically, every worker now treats the system as a threat and feeds it the minimum. You optimised measurement and destroyed the behaviour you were measuring.`,
                    coach_r2_bydesign:      `<strong>Purpose-limited AND POPIA-by-design.</strong> A coordination-and-safety purpose, restricted access, and a completed DPIA with a fixed lawful basis is the compliant default — good ethics and formal compliance, which are different things and you now have both.`,
                    coach_r2_nodpia:        `<strong>Where's your DPIA?</strong> Good internal intentions don't substitute for POPIA's formal requirements — a lawful basis, a DPIA, and a privacy notice that actually mentions the telematics. The Information Regulator can act regardless of how fair your rules feel.`,
                    coach_r2_off:           `<strong>You switched the analytics off entirely.</strong> Surveillance risk gone — but so is the fatigue-and-safety detection that could prevent a driver-fatigue crash, and most of the efficiency case with it. Did you eliminate the ethical problem, or just refuse to govern a tool with real upside?`,
                    coach_r2_ungoverned:    `<strong>Keeping full capture “and governing it later” is governing it never.</strong> Once go-live happens with everything on and no rules, the surveillance is the default and trust is already gone. Govern the dual-use system before, not after.`,
                    coach_r3_phased:        `<strong>A genuine phased rollout buys you learning.</strong> Starting with willing DCs, fixing what breaks, then scaling is how you build adoption evidence — and how you ask the board for time with a credible plan rather than a promise.`,
                    coach_r3_bigbang:       `<strong>Big-bang across all 9 DCs is momentum over trust.</strong> You'll be live everywhere before you know whether anyone is using it as intended — and a low-trust workforce hit all at once is exactly how a go-slow spreads.`,
                    coach_r3_codesign:      `<strong>Co-design is how adoption is co-produced.</strong> Consulting the union early and letting workers shape the safeguards turns a system done <em>to</em> them into one done <em>with</em> them. That's the difference between 38% and a workforce that trusts the tool.`,
                    coach_r3_noconsult:     `<strong>You rolled out without consulting.</strong> The system works perfectly and nobody uses it as intended — because it was done <em>to</em> them, not <em>with</em> them. You've handed the union a legitimate grievance for free. What would two weeks of consultation have cost versus a national dispute?`,
                    coach_r3_noinvest:      `<strong>Adoption doesn't happen because you switched the system on.</strong> With no training, no transparency and no champions, the 38% stays the 38% — and the efficiency case stays a slide, not a result.`,
                    coach_r3_overpromise:   `<strong>You committed the board to a target your levers can't support.</strong> A number far above what your consultation and data rules can carry isn't ambition, it's a future credibility loss — and when you miss it, the trust you needed to recover is the trust you just spent.`,
                    coach_r4_oversight:     `<strong>Independent oversight with worker representation has real teeth.</strong> An external eye on the algorithm and workers in governance is what makes the ethics promise binding — and what reassures the union, the regulator and the journalist at once.`,
                    coach_r4_hitl:          `<strong>Human-in-the-loop plus an appeal mechanism is enforceable ethics.</strong> No individual is disciplined by a score they can't see or contest. That is the safeguard a worker can actually rely on — symbolic charters can't say the same.`,
                    coach_r4_symbolic:      `<strong>A statement of intent with no enforcement is PR, not a safeguard.</strong> The first time a worker is disciplined by a score they can't contest, “we respect employee data” is exposed. What in your safeguard actually constrains a manager?`,
                    coach_r4_charter:       `<strong>Lovely charter on the intranet.</strong> But there's no human-in-the-loop on individual decisions, no appeal, and no one independent reviewing the algorithm. The first disciplined worker who can't see their score makes it PR. What in it is actually binding?`,
                    coach_r4_nosafeguard:   `<strong>“Good intentions are enough” is exactly what you can't evidence to a forum.</strong> Without a binding mechanism, the union has no reason to trust the data rules and the regulator has nothing to point to. Goodwill is not a safeguard.`,
                    coach_r4_underfunded:   `<strong>A binding safeguard with no funding isn't binding.</strong> An appeal nobody is staffed to hear and an oversight function nobody runs collapses the first time it's tested. Fund the teeth or they're not teeth.`,
                    coach_r4_boardonly:     `<strong>Promising the full ZAR 95M “regardless” is the trap.</strong> The number is only real if adoption is real, and adoption is only real if the union and workers trust the system. Reassuring the board while ignoring the forum is how you lose both.`,
                    coach_r4_deflect:       `<strong>“Just a productivity tool” is the line the exposé is built on.</strong> Restating it to the union and the journalist after a leak that proved otherwise reads as evasion. You don't defend an algorithmic-management story by denying it's algorithmic management.`,
                    coach_r4_defended:      `<strong>That's the integrated defence.</strong> A binding safeguard plus a case that reconciles the efficiency target, POPIA, the union and trust — that's how the ZAR 95M becomes real rather than promised, and how the forum, the regulator and the journalist all find a story that holds.`,

