                    insight_responsible:    `Responsible rollout: you governed a dual-use system as a socio-technical one — purpose-limited POPIA-by-design rules, consultation-led adoption and a binding safeguard. Trust rose, the workforce adopted, the union has nothing to dispute, and the ZAR 95M became a real result rather than a board promise.`,
                    insight_surveillance:   `Surveillance backlash: letting individual data drive performance and “right-sizing” told every worker the system was a threat. Adoption stalled, the data you collected stopped reflecting real work, and the efficiency case collapsed — you optimised measurement and destroyed the behaviour you were measuring.`,
                    insight_short:          `You didn't fully reconcile the four decisions. The data rules, the rollout and the safeguard still tell slightly different stories — enough for the union, the regulator or the journalist to find the seam, and enough to leave adoption short of the target.`,
                    insight_fin_negative:   `Your net financial outcome is negative: adoption never reached the level the ZAR 95M case assumed, so gaming, work-to-rule and reputational drag pulled you toward the ~ZAR 40M downside. The efficiency number was always an adoption number in disguise.`,
                    insight_fin_strong:     `Strong financial outcome: because adoption and trust held, the efficiency case largely realised — proof that the ZAR 95M was earned through the social subsystem, not declared in a board pack.`,
                    insight_purpose:        `You permitted individual data for performance or headcount use — a purpose limitation breach under POPIA and the fastest way to make workers treat the system as a threat. Purpose limitation wasn't a formality; it was the precondition for adoption.`,
                    insight_symbolic:       `Your ethics safeguard was symbolic — a charter with no human-in-the-loop, no enforceable appeal and no independent review. The first contested score exposes it as PR; a safeguard that doesn't constrain management isn't a safeguard.`,
                    insight_analyticsoff:   `You switched the analytics off entirely. Surveillance risk vanished — but so did the fatigue-and-safety detection and most of the efficiency case. The goal was responsible governance of a dual-use tool, not blanket avoidance of one with real upside.`,
                    insight_popia:          `POPIA compliance stayed low: a missing DPIA, an unfixed lawful basis or a privacy notice that never mentioned the telematics. Information ethics and formal data-protection compliance are distinct — and the Information Regulator needs the formal one documented.`,
                    insight_trusted:        `Trust and adoption are both high — the workforce experiences WOMS as a tool that helps them, not one that watches them. That is the social subsystem accepting the technical one, which is the whole game.`,
